# Netlib infeasible subset — 29 instances, pinned.
#
# This set asks the one question the rest of M1 cannot: given a model with no
# feasible point, does JAOS say so — and does it ever report an optimum for
# one? Everything else here checks that a right answer is recognised; this is
# the only thing checking that a wrong one is refused. Not hypothetical: the
# revert of 2026-08-07 was forced by its mirror image, a feasible instance
# (pilot-we) reported INFEASIBLE, and nothing in the suite caught the reverse.
#
#   make netlib-infeas
#
# Collected by John W. Chinneck (Carleton University), distributed at
# netlib.org/lp/infeas in netlib's packed form. bench/fetch.sh expands them
# with netlib's emps, which it downloads and checksum-verifies at fetch time
# and never stores here (PLAN 2.10, Q6).
#
# Format matches netlib.manifest so one parser serves every set:
#
#     name  sha256  rows  cols  reference  source  objconst
#
# The sha256 is of the packed file exactly as netlib serves it, before emps.
# `reference` and `objconst` are unused and written 0.0 — for this set the
# verdict IS the reference, and the runner is told so with `-e infeasible`.
# rows/cols still matter: a reader that dropped the very rows making a model
# infeasible would otherwise pass for exactly the wrong reason. They are
# counted from the expanded MPS as every row in ROWS but the objective. Free
# rows count: `greenbea` carries 111 of them beyond its objective row, and
# JAOS loads all of them, so excluding N rows wholesale gets it wrong by
# exactly that many. The shape check caught it on the first run of this set,
# which is the check doing its job on the manifest rather than on the reader.
#
# Note: `greenbea` here is NOT the greenbea of the standard set. It is the
# original, infeasible version; the feasible one was repaired from it by Bob
# Fourer. Two different models, one name, and they must not share a filename
# on disk.
#
